+    "content_html": "<p>Building wealth through monthly investing comes down to picking low-cost, diversified funds and staying consistent for decades. Here's how the top 10 stack up.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Top picks for monthly investing:</strong></p>\n\n<ol class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>FXAIX</strong> (Fidelity 500 Index) \u2013 0.015% fee, $0 min, S&amp;P 500 core holding.</li>\n\n<li><strong>SWPPX</strong> (Schwab S&amp;P 500) \u2013 0.02% fee, $1 min, near-identical to FXAIX.</li>\n\n<li><strong>FZROX</strong> (Fidelity ZERO Total Market) \u2013 0% fee, broadest US exposure.</li>\n\n<li><strong>VTSAX</strong> (Vanguard Total Stock Market) \u2013 0.04% fee, $3,000 min, the classic.</li>\n\n<li><strong>VIGAX</strong> (Vanguard Growth Index) \u2013 0.05% fee, growth tilt, higher volatility.</li>\n\n<li><strong>FCNTX</strong> (Fidelity Contrafund) \u2013 ~0.74% fee; manager Danoff retiring late 2026.</li>\n\n<li><strong>TRBCX</strong> (T. Rowe Price Blue Chip Growth) \u2013 ~0.70% fee, strong active track record.</li>\n\n<li><strong>AGTHX</strong> (Growth Fund of America) \u2013 Class A carries a sales load; best via 401(k).</li>\n\n<li><strong>VWELX</strong> (Vanguard Wellington) \u2013 65/35 balanced, gentler ride, since 1929.</li>\n\n<li><strong>VTIAX</strong> (Vanguard Total Intl) \u2013 non-US diversifier, not a standalone core fund.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p>For most beginners, FXAIX or SWPPX alone is the simplest, cheapest starting point.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Part 2</strong> covers risk data, $500/$1,000/$2,000 monthly growth projections, crash performance, and ready-made portfolios.</p>",
