+    "content_html": "<p>When you invest money for several years, simply looking at the starting and ending values does not always tell you how fast your investment actually grew.</p>\n\n<p>For example, if an investment grows from <strong>\u20b91,00,000 to \u20b92,00,000 over 5 years</strong>, the total gain is \u20b91,00,000. But what was the average annual growth rate?</p>\n\n<p>This is where <strong>CAGR (Compound Annual Growth Rate)</strong> becomes useful.</p>\n\n<p>A <strong>CAGR Calculator</strong> helps you calculate the annualized growth rate of an investment between two values over a specific period.</p>\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image\"><img src=\"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/W4mpkTcYD44jPRRh1eIz1-xrUuTpe8kxPFopeh3wcLHrmvVSTfw9rm4lUkWvVwO5D2FZz3uVCtryaKNzaTdlGDMsE2yP97UBGtcsrGTtfyrBXASpCYTlMvJK5qtYxtFYb1HLDj_rhgC-xl1E1qIU7OLoURfY9Jl_fPISr-elRdc?purpose=inline\" alt=\"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/9haUfe5gD1ueBezIzUfGSYNHF3-HXpdmnMBk0skD5df-bIpkxGAjO8t-EWiMlNIsIRnHY583CJFX2ZGuOw9JYa_049GRD9tvFbEv3KSZ7yRKKImbUaep0pi0aG68-Uvt989jnRSpfQ3d3VE5zUaVPRgYZrdjJhWDi9sGfE3Vj3zPS3_h64YAc4RTpbBduA96?purpose=fullsize\"/></figure>\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image\"><img src=\"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/YA5G9bCDiH5cIAXo-guD4e6r6nJKa0835Ckh1rZHmVMNerpV4LIxAgriLlIWHM302shS3cEs3qUB4nkplSoQU1VHkNaZEcmTpk2E7CtKObe4p5Q4EgXjKdoFr3MiI53Gefp1YLtCZAleohtyrdxI2Y91MdwdgahuSWXjZicTddM?purpose=inline\" alt=\"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/ZM2T7Sc5jV7gR9JG6d7g9eyPeJvsnrmp6GrlRGxguub0twYi_dzNTCldAW_4GpwB62qXlYXsriM09yGBfWvt3sbYxpB48DQqDg9uH65jjmW6yAect9T-Ni1ijbavU5AvhYxyddjlHiJkENoMiY6r7ZIb2yX6yPPsemHTqiG9fqVCAHUISGC1Y6sPOB0LgxIM?purpose=fullsize\"/></figure>\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image\"><img src=\"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/slfMWlS959cYfu18CqKjK5zaK8uE1rxkPnyD1z_8j_nmjWjEA-CMqHbXqdvu1QYwrzQk5hs3b19EQZfAc5NYuogXSNYBlw7CvgY9brRnPRhxlHv2Qk9k0sAyJiFk3TCsY8SfpaW3OqUhZ89T-XCl8SAZ2uEu_o-Twl2fEa1QLDM?purpose=inline\" alt=\"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/NCKI3K-rOAnW3y1sj6vAY8170dmO2ihOMsw6ko8sXO7jx6KyAuUTmqwD7x7XmkBYV_hrBSD9-61Lx2YIU27kTsHEVz-iTuLE0l298T0THwXRujgQqRrs2r14eX-Z7YM8QkkLXzEGAQ1EZR5RrhLQ3leamRNb-GCB__S8ejCXdOQgBukPgOKsBpAEMTH9QLqN?purpose=fullsize\"/></figure>\n\n<p>6</p>\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\"/>\n\n<h1 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Is CAGR?</h1>\n\n<p><strong>CAGR stands for Compound Annual Growth Rate.</strong></p>\n\n<p>It represents the annualized rate at which an investment would have grown if it had increased at a constant compounded rate every year.</p>\n\n<p>The important word here is <strong>annualized</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>CAGR converts the total growth between a beginning value and an ending value into an equivalent yearly growth rate.</p>\n\n<p>For example:</p>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Initial investment: \u20b91,00,000</li>\n\n<li>Final value: \u20b92,00,000</li>\n\n<li>Investment period: 5 years</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>The total return is <strong>100%</strong>, but the CAGR is much lower than 100% because the investment took five years to reach that value.</p>\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\"/>\n\n<h1 class=\"wp-block-heading\">CAGR Formula</h1>\n\n<p>The standard CAGR formula is:</p>\n\n<p><strong>CAGR = (Ending Value / Beginning Value)^(1 / Number of Years) \u2212 1</strong></p>\n\n<p>Where:</p>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><strong>Beginning Value</strong> = Initial investment or starting value</li>\n\n<li><strong>Ending Value</strong> = Final investment value</li>\n\n<li><strong>Number of Years</strong> = Investment period</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">FV=PV(1+r)^n</annotation></semantics></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>100000</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>5</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mtext>$161,051.00</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">FV=PV(1+r)^n=100000(1+0.1)^{5}=\\text{\\$161,051.00}</annotation></semantics></math>FV=PV(1+r)n=100000(1+0.1)5=$161,051.00</p>\n\n<p>PV = starting value; FV = future value; r = rate; n = periods</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{PV}</annotation></semantics></math>PV</p>\n\n<p>$</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{PV}</annotation></semantics></math>PV</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math>r</p>\n\n<p>%</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math>r</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math>n</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math>n</p>\n\n<p>Value ($)</p>\n\n<p></p>\n\n<p>Periods (n)</p>\n\n<p>Give feedback</p>\n\n<p>For CAGR, the formula is rearranged to find the annualized growth rate.</p>\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\"/>\n\n<h1 class=\"wp-block-heading\">CAGR Calculator Example</h1>\n\n<p>Suppose you invested:</p>\n\n<p><strong>\u20b91,00,000</strong></p>\n\n<p>and after 5 years your investment became:</p>\n\n<p><strong>\u20b91,61,051</strong></p>\n\n<p>Using the CAGR formula:</p>\n\n<p><strong>CAGR = (1,61,051 / 1,00,000)^(1/5) \u2212 1</strong></p>\n\n<p>The result is approximately:</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>10% CAGR</strong></h3>\n\n<p>This means the investment's beginning and ending values are equivalent to growing at approximately 10% annually on a compounded basis.</p>\n\n<p>It does <strong>not</strong> mean the investment actually returned exactly 10% every year.</p>\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\"/>\n\n<h1 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Does CAGR Tell You?</h1>\n\n<p>CAGR gives you a standardized way to express growth over multiple years.</p>\n\n<p>Suppose two investments have these results:</p>\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><thead><tr><th>Investment</th><th>Starting Value</th><th>Ending Value</th><th>Period</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Investment A</td><td>\u20b91 lakh</td><td>\u20b92 lakh</td><td>5 years</td></tr><tr><td>Investment B</td><td>\u20b91 lakh</td><td>\u20b92 lakh</td><td>8 years</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n\n<p>Both generated the same total increase, but their annualized growth rates are different because the investment periods are different.</p>\n\n<p>This is one reason CAGR is useful when comparing investments or business growth over different time periods.</p>\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\"/>\n\n<h1 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How to Use a CAGR Calculator</h1>\n\n<p>Using a CAGR Calculator requires just three primary inputs.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">1. Enter the Initial Value</h3>\n\n<p>Enter the amount at the beginning of the investment period.</p>\n\n<p>Example:</p>\n\n<p><strong>\u20b91,00,000</strong></p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">2. Enter the Final Value</h3>\n\n<p>Enter the value at the end of the period.</p>\n\n<p>Example:</p>\n\n<p><strong>\u20b92,00,000</strong></p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">3. Enter the Investment Period</h3>\n\n<p>Enter the number of years.</p>\n\n<p>Example:</p>\n\n<p><strong>5 years</strong></p>\n\n<p>The calculator then calculates the annualized growth rate.</p>\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\"/>\n\n<h1 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why Use a CAGR Calculator?</h1>\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">1. Measure Investment Growth</h2>\n\n<p>CAGR allows you to understand how quickly an investment grew on an annualized basis.</p>\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">2. Compare Investments</h2>\n\n<p>You can compare investments that were held for different periods by converting their growth into annualized rates.</p>\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">3. Analyze Business Growth</h2>\n\n<p>CAGR is also widely used to describe growth in:</p>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Revenue</li>\n\n<li>Sales</li>\n\n<li>Customers</li>\n\n<li>Assets</li>\n\n<li>Market size</li>\n\n<li>Profits</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">4. Understand Long-Term Performance</h2>\n\n<p>A simple percentage gain does not show how long it took to achieve that gain.</p>\n\n<p>CAGR adds the time dimension.</p>\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">5. Save Calculation Time</h2>\n\n<p>Instead of manually applying the formula, a CAGR Calculator can produce the result quickly.</p>\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\"/>\n\n<h1 class=\"wp-block-heading\">CAGR vs Total Return</h1>\n\n<p>These two terms should not be confused.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Total Return</h3>\n\n<p>Total return measures the overall increase or decrease between the starting and ending values.</p>\n\n<p>For example:</p>\n\n<p>\u20b91,00,000 \u2192 \u20b91,50,000</p>\n\n<p>Total growth:</p>\n\n<p><strong>50%</strong></p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">CAGR</h3>\n\n<p>CAGR annualizes that growth based on the investment period.</p>\n\n<p>If the \u20b91,00,000 became \u20b91,50,000 over 5 years, the CAGR would be approximately <strong>8.45%</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>So:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Total Return = Overall Growth</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>CAGR = Annualized Growth Rate</strong></p>\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\"/>\n\n<h1 class=\"wp-block-heading\">CAGR vs Annual Return</h1>\n\n<p>CAGR assumes a constant compounded annual growth rate for the purpose of representing the beginning and ending values.</p>\n\n<p>Actual investments generally do not grow at a perfectly constant rate.</p>\n\n<p>For example, an investment might produce:</p>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Year 1: +18%</li>\n\n<li>Year 2: \u22125%</li>\n\n<li>Year 3: +12%</li>\n\n<li>Year 4: +7%</li>\n\n<li>Year 5: +15%</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>The CAGR summarizes the overall beginning-to-ending growth into one annualized figure.</p>\n\n<p>Therefore, <strong>CAGR should not be interpreted as proof that the investment earned that exact percentage every year.</strong></p>\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\"/>\n\n<h1 class=\"wp-block-heading\">CAGR vs XIRR</h1>\n\n<p>CAGR works particularly well when you have a <strong>single starting value, a single ending value and a defined time period</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>XIRR is more appropriate when there are <strong>multiple cash flows occurring on different dates</strong>.</p>\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-table\"><table class=\"has-fixed-layout\"><thead><tr><th>CAGR</th><th>XIRR</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Single beginning and ending value</td><td>Multiple cash flows</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed investment period</td><td>Actual transaction dates</td></tr><tr><td>Annualized growth rate</td><td>Annualized return based on cash-flow timing</td></tr><tr><td>Simple performance comparison</td><td>Useful for irregular investments/withdrawals</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n\n<p>For investments involving multiple deposits or withdrawals, CAGR may not adequately represent the investor's actual return experience.</p>\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\"/>\n\n<h1 class=\"wp-block-heading\">CAGR Example: Stock Investment</h1>\n\n<p>Suppose you purchased shares worth:</p>\n\n<p><strong>\u20b92,00,000</strong></p>\n\n<p>After 4 years, their value became:</p>\n\n<p><strong>\u20b93,00,000</strong></p>\n\n<p>The total gain is:</p>\n\n<p><strong>\u20b91,00,000</strong></p>\n\n<p>or:</p>\n\n<p><strong>50% total growth</strong></p>\n\n<p>But the CAGR is approximately:</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\"><strong>10.67%</strong></h3>\n\n<p>This provides a more useful annualized representation of the growth over those four years.</p>\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\"/>\n\n<h1 class=\"wp-block-heading\">CAGR Example: Business Revenue</h1>\n\n<p>CAGR is not limited to investments.</p>\n\n<p>Suppose a company's revenue was:</p>\n\n<p><strong>\u20b910 crore in 2020</strong></p>\n\n<p>and became:</p>\n\n<p><strong>\u20b925 crore in 2025</strong></p>\n\n<p>The CAGR can be used to express the company's annualized revenue growth over the five-year period.</p>\n\n<p>This makes CAGR particularly useful in:</p>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Business analysis</li>\n\n<li>Market research</li>\n\n<li>Financial reports</li>\n\n<li>Investment analysis</li>\n\n<li>Strategic planning</li>\n</ul>\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\"/>\n\n<h1 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What Can Affect CAGR?</h1>\n\n<p>The CAGR result depends on three things:</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Starting Value</h3>\n\n<p>A different beginning value changes the calculation.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Ending Value</h3>\n\n<p>The final value directly affects the calculated growth rate.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Time Period</h3>\n\n<p>The longer the period, the more important the time component becomes.</p>\n\n<p>For the same beginning and ending values, changing the number of years changes the CAGR.</p>\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\"/>\n\n<h1 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Limitations of CAGR</h1>\n\n<p>CAGR is useful, but it has limitations.</p>\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">CAGR Hides Volatility</h2>\n\n<p>CAGR does not show the ups and downs experienced during the investment period.</p>\n\n<p>An investment could have experienced significant losses and gains but still end with the same CAGR as an investment with smoother returns.</p>\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">CAGR Does Not Show Cash Flows</h2>\n\n<p>If you regularly add or withdraw money, CAGR may not accurately represent your personal investment return.</p>\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">CAGR Is Not a Forecast</h2>\n\n<p>A historical CAGR does not guarantee the same growth rate in the future.</p>\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">CAGR Does Not Include Everything Automatically</h2>\n\n<p>Depending on the data being analyzed, taxes, fees, dividends, additional investments and withdrawals may need separate treatment.</p>\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\"/>\n\n<h1 class=\"wp-block-heading\">When Should You Use a CAGR Calculator?</h1>\n\n<p>A CAGR Calculator can be particularly useful when you want to:</p>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Calculate historical investment growth</li>\n\n<li>Compare two investment results</li>\n\n<li>Analyze business revenue growth</li>\n\n<li>Measure asset appreciation</li>\n\n<li>Study market growth</li>\n\n<li>Understand annualized performance</li>\n\n<li>Analyze long-term financial data</li>\n</ul>\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\"/>\n\n<h1 class=\"wp-block-heading\">How CAGR Helps in Investment Analysis</h1>\n\n<p>Imagine two investments:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Investment A:</strong><br>\u20b91 lakh \u2192 \u20b91.8 lakh in 5 years</p>\n\n<p><strong>Investment B:</strong><br>\u20b91 lakh \u2192 \u20b91.8 lakh in 8 years</p>\n\n<p>Looking only at the final amount makes both investments appear identical.</p>\n\n<p>CAGR introduces the missing variable:</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Time</h3>\n\n<p>That makes annualized comparison much more meaningful.</p>\n\n<p>However, when comparing real-world investments, you should also consider risk, volatility, taxes, fees and cash-flow timing rather than relying on CAGR alone.</p>\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\"/>\n\n<h1 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Frequently Asked Questions</h1>\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What is a CAGR Calculator?</h2>\n\n<p>A CAGR Calculator calculates the compound annual growth rate between a starting value and ending value over a specified period.</p>\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">What is the CAGR formula?</h2>\n\n<p>The standard formula is:</p>\n\n<p><strong>CAGR = (Ending Value / Beginning Value)^(1 / Years) \u2212 1</strong></p>\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Is CAGR the same as annual return?</h2>\n\n<p>No. CAGR is an annualized growth rate calculated from the beginning and ending values. Actual annual returns can vary significantly from year to year.</p>\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Can CAGR be negative?</h2>\n\n<p>Yes. If the ending value is lower than the beginning value, the calculated CAGR will be negative.</p>\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Can CAGR be more than 100%?</h2>\n\n<p>Yes. Depending on the starting value, ending value and period, CAGR can exceed 100%.</p>\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Can CAGR be used for SIP investments?</h2>\n\n<p>CAGR is generally designed around a beginning value and ending value. For investments involving regular cash flows such as SIPs, <strong>XIRR</strong> is generally more appropriate for measuring the investor's annualized return because it accounts for the timing of individual cash flows.</p>\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Is CAGR a guaranteed return?</h2>\n\n<p>No. CAGR is a calculation based on historical or specified beginning and ending values. It does not guarantee future performance.</p>\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\"/>\n\n<h1 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Calculate CAGR with Numrexo</h1>\n\n<p>Understanding your investment's growth rate is an important part of financial analysis.</p>\n\n<p>Instead of manually calculating powers, divisions and annualized rates, you can use the <strong>Numrexo CAGR Calculator</strong> to quickly calculate CAGR from your beginning value, ending value and investment period.</p>\n\n<p>Explore the <a href=\"https://numrexo.com/investment?utm_source=chatgpt.com\">Numrexo Investment Calculators</a>.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Related Calculators</h3>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li><a>CAGR Calculator</a></li>\n\n<li><a>Lumpsum Calculator</a></li>\n\n<li><a href=\"https://numrexo.com/investment/sip-calculator\">SIP Calculator</a></li>\n\n<li><a>XIRR Calculator</a></li>\n\n<li><a>CAPM Calculator</a></li>\n\n<li><a href=\"https://numrexo.com/investment?utm_source=chatgpt.com\">Investment Calculator Hub</a></li>\n</ul>\n\n<hr class=\"wp-block-separator has-alpha-channel-opacity\"/>\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">SEO Details</h2>\n\n<p><strong>Meta Title:</strong><br>CAGR Calculator \u2013 Calculate Compound Annual Growth Rate</p>\n\n<p><strong>Meta Description:</strong><br>Use the free CAGR Calculator to calculate the annualized growth rate of an investment using its starting value, final value and investment period.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Focus Keyphrase:</strong><br>CAGR Calculator</p>\n\n<p><strong>Secondary Keywords:</strong></p>\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>CAGR Calculator India</li>\n\n<li>CAGR calculator online</li>\n\n<li>Compound Annual Growth Rate calculator</li>\n\n<li>CAGR formula</li>\n\n<li>investment CAGR calculator</li>\n\n<li>CAGR return 